Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:05.829 align:start position:0%
 
hello everyone it's my honor to  introduce our efforts on building a  unified api wrapper to simplify the web  data collection  my name is pae wong i'm a senior phd  student in sfu vancouver  i do research on data management  problems and my colleague william who is  going to show the demo  is also a phd student and he enjoys  contributing to open source project  okay let's get started data collection  is a critical step in data science  lifecycle  web apis are the interface provided by  websites  as a way to access their databases in a  secure manner  web apis make it possible to access rich  data resources  that cover many domains it's very common  to collect data with web apis  here i listed a few example apis that  many of us  are very familiar with the right hand  side is a very popular github repo  that collects many public apis and here  i just  show a part of the domains that those  api cover you can imagine how much data  behind those apis  although a big value accessing such data  resources can require  advanced programming skills to  facilitate the individual users  there are many popular open source api  wrappers in github they basically wrap  up complex api calls  into easy to use python python functions  however to build such a wrapper is  challenging a functioning wrapper needs  to  tackle a few key components first  component would be to build the http  connection with the website server  second to handle the authorization  authorization for the user  so that the user can just pass the  credentials as the function parameters  and pagination which is to combine data  requested from multiple pages into one  function call  concurrency is to accelerate the data  request process with parallelism  implementation  and the wrapper also handles the result  parsing such as converting json results  to a csv file or pandas data frame  there might be other customized  customized ones  and building wrappers for each website  is not only difficult but also not  scalable  what i mean is that existing wrappers  are built in a web specific way without  considering their commonalities  in this way every time the developers  would build a wrapper for a new website  they will need to implement those key  components over and over again  that's huge repetitive engineering  efforts  from the user's perspective because  those those wrappers are customized for  the websites  and uh implemented by different  developers the user will have to learn  how to use a new wrapper  over and over again as well  another way our our vision is to unify  the api rapid building process  so that it saves energy for both  developers and users  and our library is called  dataprep.connector the key idea is to  implement the general components  that are shared among all websites only  once and described  the website's specific information in  configuration files  such as what authorization and the  pagination scheme the website uses  for developers building a new wrapper is  just to write a new configuration file  from the user's perspective they can  query different websites with a very  simple and similar syntax  when they call the functions the library  reads from the configuration file  assemble the request and execute here is  an example of how to search the machine  learning papers in dblp  users users only need to provide the  minimal input like authorization tokens  and query keywords  the process to query the spotify is  pretty much similar  here is how we connect to spotify and  corridor album search api  and the next we will do the demo  thanks pay next i'm going to show you  two features of the connector  it's easy to use in that you can collect  data from a website in three lines of  code  it's also fast to you in that you can  collect data from websites in parallel  now let's imagine we want to collect  data from a website called dbrp  ebrp is a computer science bibliography  website contains publications authors  and venue information  this is a website of dbrp on this  website you can see  the search box here you can input search  keywords to do search for example i can  type in  slightly learning this will return you  the publications that are related to  this search keyword  you can see here it returns 21 matches  of this search  i can also search for cbpr 2020 which is  a computer science  conference focusing on computer vision  now for this search i get 1996 matches  think about what if you want to get the  search data using python  luckily dbrp provides you an api page  which contains three api endpoints  publication search authors search  and manual search it also contains a  bunch of parameters to be  issued along with requests to these api  endpoints  for example you have a parameter queue  for which you can put the query string  in it  for example in our case we can put  cvpr2020 into  s parameter queue it also has parameter  edge which indicates the maximum number  of search results to return for each  request  by default it's 30. with all this being  given it's still not easy  to create python code to issue http  requests and get data  this is because you still need to  manually compose http request  for example you can use a python request  library to do that and also you still  need to pass the response from the  returned neurologist  let's see how we can use connector to  simplify this process  here i first import the connector  function from the data package  and then i use a connect with a  parameter dbrp to create a connector to  the dbrp website  finally i use the query function on this  connector object  with the parameter publication indicates  i want to query the publication endpoint  and also i pass in the search term  cbpr2020  if i run this query it will finish in  one  second let's also take a peek at  the results we get  it's appendix data frame and you can see  all these results inside the data frame  since the query function gives you a  pandas data frame you can directly apply  analysis workflow you are familiar with  on it using pandas  or just persists the data to disk also  notice the highlighted await  at the beginning this is because the  query function returns something lazy  evaluated  applying a weight on that result will  execute the lazy value and returns user  result  connector uses this feature to achieve  parallelism which i will show you later  now let's check out how many records we  gathered  it's 30 instead of 1996 as we saw on the  website  remember that by default ebrp only  returns 30 records per request  but with connector you can also specify  the desired record number you want to  get by specifying the count argument  now i'm going to add a count here  and give it 2000 as the value i run it  again  you see i get exactly 1996 records  this is one of the coolest features of  the connector because this enables us to  get any amount of data without bothering  to understand the api detail  this is because actually dbrp limits the  records to return for each query as 1000  but since we specify 2000 here connector  knows that  it should break it down to two query  instead of one and send them out  simultaneously  after receiving all the responses it  will concatenate the tables into a  single data frame  next i'm going to show you how to enable  the connector to issue requests in  parallel  this time i want to see more papers  related to cppr  what i'm going to do is first copy the  code to here  and then i remove the 2020 from the  query term  and then i increase count to 10 000.  around the query you can notice that  this query executes a little bit slower  this is because we increase the count we  want to get from the  front of the query and this means we  need to issue more requests to the  underlying api  to elevate this problem we also have the  concurrency parameter here  to accelerate the query if you have lots  of requests to send out  by default the setting is one which  means connector will send out the  request  one per second but i if i increase it to  10  it will send out 10 requests in one  second  you see now it takes less than four  seconds to get the data  at this point you might wonder what if  you have a new website how do you add it  to our connector  it's actually fairly easy you only need  to create a config file for that  and then add it to our ripple you can  see this is our ripple  we currently have think hub we have  spotify we have twitter  and other websites besides dbrp and  yield  you can also take a look at the content  of the configuration file  inside the configuration file you mainly  have two sections  one is request another is response the  request section  describes the information that connector  requires to  send the request to the underlying api  for example it contains the uri  the method of the http request and  authorization scheme and other steps  inside the response section it describes  the schema information  which connector will use it to convert  the response into appendage data frame  to further simplify the config creation  we also provide a ui to generate the  configuration file semi automatically  using these two lines of code it will  display a generator ui  inside the jupyter notebook this  generator takes a request  example and it will return your  configuration file  for example you can type in the url of  the request  and also the request parameters and  other information is required to send  out a request  and then click this button the generator  will send out the request and get the  response  and then it will pass the response to  get the schema of the underlying table  and then generates a configuration file  for you after that  you can just use this generated  configuration file to  to send out the requests using the  connector or even better submit to our  ripple so that the community can benefit  from it  that's it i will give a swing back to  pay  okay thanks for watching connector is a  part of the dataprep library  currently we are also developing a few  other cool components such as eda and  cleaning  feel free to check out and finally to  summarize  dataprep.connector is an open source api  wrapper that make web api access easy  it speeds up rapid development process  for developers  and also standardized cost to multiple  apis  as a simple workflow for the users the  project will be possible with our  fantastic contributors  don't forget to start us on github and  give it a try thank you

